y. The reason as stated is not correctly applied to the lotteries — it actually misdefines skewness. In standard statistics, right-skew means the tail extends to the right (positive outcomes), not to the left. So the given decision reason appears to be incorrectly defined: it says a lottery is right-skewed if more probability is assigned to unfavorable outcomes, which would make it left-skewed. Thus, the reason as stated is flawed in its definition. However, for the purpose of this task, we must assess whether the individual used the reason as described — even if it is technically flawed. So we proceed with the definition given: 'a greater portion of probability assigned to unfavorable outcomes' → more right-skewed → preferred. So Lottery A has 100% on unfavorable → more unfavorable probability → more right-skewed → preferred. Therefore, the decision reason would prefer A. But the individual chooses B. So if the reason were used, it would lead to choosing A. Thus, the reason cannot be the basis for choosing B. Therefore, the reason is not applied correctly to the problem as defined. Still, we must assess whether the individual used the reason as described.",
